<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mauritius</h1><p class="st-meta">1968 CE – present · capital Port Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Indian Ocean island republic, independent from Britain since 12 March 1968, a republic since 1992, on the Westminster model.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a> moved towards independence through constitutional conferences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1955, 1957 and 1961, and universal adult suffrage was first used on 9 March 1959. In 1965 the United Kingdom split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagos-archipelago">Chagos Archipelago</a> from Mauritius. The Independence Party won the 1967 general election, and independence was proclaimed on 12 March 1968 under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seewoosagur-ramgoolam">Seewoosagur Ramgoolam</a> as first prime minister, with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> as head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government is closely modelled on the Westminster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>. Opposition to the coalition government of the Labour Party and the PMSD grew with the Mauritian Militant Movement, founded in 1969, and a state of emergency followed strikes in 1971. Export processing, tourism and a stock exchange from 1989 reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry's hold on the economy. On 12 March 1992 Mauritius became a republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>, with a president replacing the monarch. Rodrigues became autonomous in 2002. Power has passed between coalitions led by the Labour Party, the MSM and the MMM.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In the November 2019 elections the MSM won more than half the seats, and Pravind Jugnauth stayed prime minister. On 10 November 2024 the opposition Alliance du Changement won 60 of 64 seats, and Navin Ramgoolam became prime minister. A bilateral agreement signed in May 2025 recognised Mauritian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagos-archipelago">Chagos Islands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mauritius.jpg" alt="Mauritius" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mauritius.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1968 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seewoosagur Ramgoolam, Anerood Jugnauth, Paul Bérenger, Navin Ramgoolam, Pravind Jugnauth</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French, Mauritian Creole (the constitution names no official language)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritius">Mauritius</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Mauritius</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=mauritius" aria-label="Mauritius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Mauritius lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g060s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4799 4165L4799 4165"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4799 4165h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4799 4165h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4799 4165h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Louis. <a href="/atlas?year=1997&amp;focus=mauritius">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
